@inproceedings{Hou2025,
   abstract = {On January 1, 2024, an earthquake struck the Noto Peninsula in Japan. This was the first earthquake with a magnitude of MW ≥7 in the Sea of Japan since the 1993 Hokkaido Nansei-Oki Mw 7.6 earthquake and one of the largest intraplate earthquakes in Japan's recorded history. Investigating the seismogenic fault, source mechanism, co-seismic displacement, and rupture distribution of this event is of great importance. This study utilized 25 pre-earthquake and 26 post-earthquake descending-track Sentinel-1 images, two descending-track ALOS-2 images, and Sentinel-2 optical remote sensing data covering the Noto Peninsula. Combining Synthetic Aperture Radar Interferometry (InSAR) and Pixel Offset Tracking (POT) techniques, co-seismic deformation fields in different directions across the affected region were derived.The results indicate that this earthquake was a thrust faulting event with a strike-slip component. The deformation map obtained using ALOS-2 data via InSAR showed that the maximum deformation was located in the offshore area near Wajima City, with a Line-Of-Sight (LOS) displacement of up to 1.2 meters. SAR image offset results revealed LOS deformation reaching 3 meters. By averaging the pre- and post-earthquake Sentinel-1 intensity data, the results indicated a maximum range-direction displacement of 4 meters near the epicenter, while the maximum azimuthal displacement of 1 meter was observed southwest of Wajima City. Sentinel-2 results showed horizontal displacements reaching up to 2 meters, with an overall westward deformation trend. ©2025 IEEE.},
